CAT = "s65TaxRetire"; SEG = "TAX"
Q = [
('A client bought a fund for $14,000 eleven months ago and sells it today for $17,500. The gain is:', 'Short term, at ordinary rates', ['Long term, at capital gains rates', 'Tax-free if reinvested', 'Long term, since it beat inflation'], 'Assets held one year or less produce short-term gains, taxed at ordinary income rates. Waiting past one year would have made the $3,500 gain long term.'),
('A father gives his daughter stock he bought for $8,000. It is worth $31,000 on the day of the gift. Her cost basis for figuring a gain is:', '$8,000', ['$31,000', '$23,000', '$19,500'], "A gift of appreciated property carries over the donor's basis, so her basis is his $8,000 and the $23,000 of built-in gain is taxed when she sells. Had she inherited the stock instead, her basis would step up to its value at his death."),
('An aunt paid $40,000 for stock and gives it to her niece when it is worth $30,000. The niece later sells it for $26,000. Her deductible loss is:', '$4,000', ['$14,000', '$10,000', 'None'], "When gifted property is worth less than the donor's basis, the basis for figuring a loss is the value on the gift date, $30,000. $30,000 - $26,000 = $4,000; the aunt's $10,000 drop is never deductible."),
('Which client is most likely to owe alternative minimum tax?', 'One with large ISO exercises', ['One with only wage income', 'One with only Roth withdrawals', 'One with a small pension'], 'The ISO spread at exercise is an AMT preference item, so large exercises often trigger AMT even though regular tax is zero on them.'),
('A client turned 73 in 2026 and holds a traditional IRA. Under SECURE 2.0, she must:', 'Begin taking RMDs', ['Close the account', 'Convert it all to a Roth', 'Stop all contributions'], 'SECURE 2.0 set the RMD starting age at 73 for people born from 1951 through 1959. Missing an RMD triggers an excise tax on the amount not taken.'),
("IRMAA, the income-related monthly adjustment amount, raises a client's:", 'Medicare premiums', ['Social Security benefits', 'IRA contribution limit', 'Estate tax exemption'], 'Higher-income Medicare enrollees pay IRMAA surcharges on Part B and Part D premiums, based on income from two years earlier. A large IRA withdrawal or Roth conversion can trigger it.'),
('A married client leaves her entire estate to her husband, a U.S. citizen. The federal estate tax due is generally:', 'Zero, under the marital deduction', ['Due on the excess over her exemption', 'Due within nine months in full', 'Paid by the husband at a lower rate'], 'The unlimited marital deduction lets a spouse pass any amount to a U.S. citizen spouse free of estate tax. Portability can then pass her unused exclusion to him.'),
('Portability in the federal estate tax lets a surviving spouse:', 'Claim the unused exclusion', ['Avoid probate entirely', 'Inherit an IRA as her own tax-free', 'Skip all gift tax returns'], "Portability lets the survivor add the deceased spouse's unused exclusion amount (DSUE) to her own, if elected on a timely estate tax return."),
('A client wants tax-deductible contributions now and will be in a much lower bracket in retirement. Which account fits best?', 'Traditional IRA', ['Roth IRA', 'Taxable brokerage account', 'Coverdell ESA'], 'Deducting contributions at a high rate and paying tax on withdrawals at a lower rate later favors a traditional IRA. A Roth wins when the later rate is higher.'),
('Which is true of a Roth IRA for its original owner?', 'It has no lifetime RMDs', ['Contributions are tax-deductible', 'Earnings are always taxable', 'RMDs begin at age 73'], 'Roth IRA owners never have to take RMDs during their lifetime. Qualified withdrawals of earnings are tax-free after five years and age 59 1/2.'),
('A self-employed consultant with no employees wants to shelter as much income as possible and allow Roth contributions. A good fit is:', 'A solo 401(k)', ['A SIMPLE IRA', 'A 403(b) plan', 'A 457 plan'], 'A solo 401(k) lets the owner contribute both as employee (pre-tax or Roth) and as employer, which usually allows more than a SEP for the same income.'),
('A 52-year-old leaves his job with a state government and takes money from its 457(b) plan. The 10% early withdrawal penalty:', 'Does not apply', ['Applies until age 59 1/2', 'Applies unless he rolls it over', 'Applies to half the amount'], 'Distributions from a governmental 457(b) plan after separation from service are not subject to the 10% early withdrawal penalty, though they are taxed as income.'),
("An executive's nonqualified deferred compensation plan is unfunded. If the company goes bankrupt, the executive is:", 'An unsecured creditor', ['Protected by ERISA', 'Insured by the PBGC', 'Paid before bondholders'], 'Nonqualified plans escape most ERISA rules, so deferred amounts stay company assets. In bankruptcy the executive stands in line with other unsecured creditors.'),
("Under ERISA Section 404(c), a 401(k) sponsor can limit its liability for participants' losses if it:", 'Offers broad choices and data', ['Guarantees a minimum return', "Picks every participant's funds", 'Offers only company stock'], '404(c) protection requires a broad range of investment options, enough information to choose, and the ability to change investments regularly.'),
('A participant never chooses investments in her 401(k). A qualified default investment alternative might be:', 'A target-date fund', ["The employer's own stock", 'A sector fund', 'A leveraged ETF'], 'QDIAs include target-date funds, balanced funds and managed accounts. A single stock or narrow fund is not a qualified default.'),
('Which act is a prohibited transaction under ERISA?', 'The plan lending money to the sponsor', ['Buying a diversified index fund', 'Paying reasonable plan expenses', "Rolling over a departing worker's balance"], 'ERISA bars most dealings between a plan and a party in interest, such as loans to the sponsoring employer, because they invite conflicts of interest.'),
('A grandparent opens a 529 plan for a grandchild. Which statement is true?', 'The account owner keeps control', ['The child owns it at age 18', 'Withdrawals are always taxable', 'Only tuition can be paid tax-free'], 'In a 529 plan the owner, not the beneficiary, controls the money and can change beneficiaries. Qualified withdrawals for tuition, fees, books and room and board are tax-free.'),
('A parent gifts shares to a child under a UTMA account. The gift:', 'Is irrevocable', ['Can be taken back later', 'Is controlled by the child now', 'Avoids all tax on income'], 'UTMA and UGMA gifts belong to the minor at once; the custodian manages them until the age set by state law. Unearned income can be taxed under the kiddie tax.'),
('A client with a high-deductible health plan funds an HSA. Withdrawals for qualified medical expenses are:', 'Tax-free', ['Taxed as ordinary income', 'Penalized before age 65', 'Taxed as capital gains'], 'HSAs offer a deduction going in, tax-free growth, and tax-free withdrawals for medical costs. After 65, non-medical withdrawals are taxed but not penalized.'),
('A married couple owns a brokerage account as joint tenants with right of survivorship. When the husband dies, his share:', 'Passes to his wife outside probate', ['Goes to the heirs named in his will', 'Is split among his children', 'Goes through probate first'], 'JTWROS property passes automatically to the surviving owner by operation of law, regardless of the will. Tenants in common shares would pass under the will instead.'),
("Which form of joint ownership is open only to married couples and, in many states, shields the property from one spouse's separate creditors?", 'Tenancy by the entirety', ['Tenancy in common', 'Joint tenancy with survivorship', 'Community property'], 'Tenancy by the entirety is limited to spouses, includes survivorship, and in many states protects the asset from creditors of only one spouse.'),
('A client wants her brokerage account to go straight to her son at death without probate, while she keeps full control now. She should use:', 'A TOD registration', ['A UTMA account for the son', 'Joint tenancy with her son', 'A gift of the shares today'], 'A TOD registration names a beneficiary who receives the account at death outside probate, while the owner keeps sole ownership during life. Joint tenancy would give the son rights now.'),
("A client's will leaves her estate to her children per stirpes. One child died before her, leaving two children. Those grandchildren:", "Share their parent's portion", ['Receive nothing', "Each get a full child's share", 'Split the whole estate equally'], "Per stirpes passes a deceased beneficiary's share down to that person's descendants, so the two grandchildren split their parent's portion."),
('A client wants a charitable deduction this year but to choose the charities over several years. A fitting vehicle is:', 'A donor-advised fund', ['A private family trust', 'A UTMA account', 'A 529 plan'], 'Contributions to a donor-advised fund are deductible when made; the sponsor owns the assets, and the donor recommends grants to charities over time.'),
]
